  "answer":"After a significant drift is detected, the runbook specifies that the next step is to trigger challenger model training and evaluation; a control batch must be evaluated before any alerts are trusted, but the drift itself does not cause an immediate model promotion. Promotion of a new model is only allowed when the release policy’s quantitative criteria are met – the new model must improve F1 score by at least 0.005 and its ROC‑AUC may not drop by more than 0.01 – and the promotion must be approved by the project owner. A drift alert alone never authorizes promotion.",
  "sources":[
    {
      "source_id":"drift-runbook",
      "quote":"Significant drift means absolute mean MonthlyCharges shift exceeds 15 USD or absolute churn-rate shift exceeds 0.05. Drift triggers challenger training and evaluation, not immediate promotion. A control batch is evaluated before trusting alerts."
    },
    {
      "source_id":"release-policy",
      "quote":"Production model changes require an evaluation on a frozen holdout. Promote only when F1 improves by at least 0.005 and ROC-AUC drops by no more than 0.01. A drift alert alone never authorizes promotion. Keep the previous production version for rollback. The release approver is the project owner."
    }
  ]
}
